::ALG2_U03_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2/x + 5/x. {
= 7/x, x &ne; 0#Correct. Common denominators stay the same while numerators add.
~ 7/(2x)#Do not add matching denominators.
~ 10/x, x &ne; 0#The operation is addition, not multiplication.
~ 7/x, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 8/(x + 1) - 3/(x + 1). {
~ 11/(x + 1), x &ne; -1#The operation is subtraction.
= 5/(x + 1), x &ne; -1#Correct. Subtract numerators over the common denominator.
~ 5/(2x + 2)#The denominator is not doubled.
~ 5/(x + 1), no restriction#The denominator excludes x = -1.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Find the LCD of 1/(x - 2) and 3/(x + 5). {
~ 2x + 3#Denominators are not added.
~ x - 2#This misses x + 5.
= (x - 2)(x + 5)#Correct. Both distinct denominator factors are needed.
~ x + 5#This misses x - 2.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/x + 1/(x + 2). {
~ 2/(2x + 2)#Denominators are not added.
~ (x + 2)/(x(x + 2)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -2#The second numerator contributes x.
~ (2x + 2)/(x + 2), x &ne; -2#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
= (2x + 2)/(x(x + 2)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -2#Correct. Use LCD x(x + 2), then combine x + 2 + x.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 4/(x - 1) - 2/(x + 1). {
= (2x + 6)/((x - 1)(x + 1)), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; -1#Correct. Use the LCD and compute 4(x + 1) - 2(x - 1).
~ (2x + 2)/((x - 1)(x + 1)), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; -1#The subtraction must distribute to -2(x - 1).
~ 2/(2x), x &ne; 0#Denominators are not added.
~ (6x)/((x - 1)(x + 1)), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; -1#Constants must be combined correctly.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 3/(x + 4) + 1/(x + 4). {
~ 4/(2x + 8)#The common denominator is not added.
= 4/(x + 4), x &ne; -4#Correct. Add numerators over the same denominator.
~ 3/(x + 4), x &ne; -4#The second numerator must be included.
~ 4/(x + 4), no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -4.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2/x - 1/(x + 1). {
~ (x - 2)/(x(x + 1)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -1#The signs in the numerator are not combined correctly.
~ 1/(2x + 1)#Denominators are not added.
= (x + 2)/(x(x + 1)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -1#Correct. Use LCD x(x + 1), then compute 2(x + 1) - x.
~ (x + 2)/(x + 1), x &ne; -1#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What restrictions apply to 1/(x - 3) + 2/x? {
~ x &ne; -3 and x &ne; 0#The sign for x - 3 is reversed.
~ x &ne; 3 only#The denominator x also excludes 0.
~ x &ne; 0 only#The denominator x - 3 also excludes 3.
= x &ne; 3 and x &ne; 0#Correct. Each original denominator cannot equal zero.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 5/(x - 6) + 2/(x - 6). {
= 7/(x - 6), x &ne; 6#Correct. Add numerators over the common denominator.
~ 10/(x - 6), x &ne; 6#The numerators should be added, not multiplied.
~ 7/(2x - 12)#The denominator is not doubled.
~ 7/(x - 6), no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 6.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/(x - 1) + 1/(x + 1). {
~ 2/((x - 1)(x + 1)), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; -1#The x terms do not cancel; they add to 2x.
= 2x/((x - 1)(x + 1)), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; -1#Correct. The numerator is (x + 1) + (x - 1) = 2x.
~ 2/(2x)#Denominators are not added.
~ 2x/(x + 1), x &ne; -1#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 7/x - 2/x. {
~ 9/x, x &ne; 0#The operation is subtraction.
~ 5/(2x)#The denominator is not doubled.
= 5/x, x &ne; 0#Correct. Subtract numerators over the common denominator.
~ 5/x, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Find the LCD of 3/(x<sup>2</sup> - 9) and 1/(x - 3). {
~ x - 3#This misses the factor x + 3.
~ x + 3#This misses the factor x - 3.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 9#The denominator should be factored as a difference of squares.
= (x - 3)(x + 3)#Correct. Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 9 as (x - 3)(x + 3).
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2/(x + 5) - 4/(x + 5). {
= -2/(x + 5), x &ne; -5#Correct. Subtract numerators: 2 - 4 = -2.
~ 6/(x + 5), x &ne; -5#The operation is subtraction.
~ -2/(2x + 10)#The common denominator is not added.
~ -2/(x + 5), no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -5.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 3/x + 2/(x - 4). {
~ (5x + 12)/(x(x - 4)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; 4#The -12 comes from 3(x - 4).
= (5x - 12)/(x(x - 4)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; 4#Correct. Use LCD x(x - 4), then compute 3(x - 4) + 2x.
~ 5/(2x - 4)#Denominators are not added.
~ (5x - 12)/(x - 4), x &ne; 4#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/(x + 2) + 3/(x - 2). {
~ (4x - 4)/((x + 2)(x - 2)), x &ne; -2 and x &ne; 2#The sign from 3(x + 2) is positive.
~ 4/(2x)#Denominators are not added.
= (4x + 4)/((x + 2)(x - 2)), x &ne; -2 and x &ne; 2#Correct. Use the LCD and compute (x - 2) + 3(x + 2).
~ (4x + 4)/(x - 2), x &ne; 2#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which step is needed before adding 1/(x + 1) and 1/(x + 4)? {
~ add the denominators#Denominators are not added.
~ cancel x from both denominators#x is not a common factor of each denominator.
~ multiply the numerators only#The denominators must also be adjusted.
= rewrite both with denominator (x + 1)(x + 4)#Correct. Different denominators require a common denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 6/(x - 2) - 1/(x - 2). {
= 5/(x - 2), x &ne; 2#Correct. Subtract numerators over the common denominator.
~ 7/(x - 2), x &ne; 2#The operation is subtraction.
~ 5/(2x - 4)#The denominator is not doubled.
~ 5/(x - 2), no restriction#The denominator excludes x = 2.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2/(x + 3) + x/(x + 3). {
~ 2x/(x + 3), x &ne; -3#Numerators add to x + 2, not 2x.
= (x + 2)/(x + 3), x &ne; -3#Correct. Add numerators over the common denominator.
~ (x + 2)/(2x + 6)#The denominator is not doubled.
~ (x + 2)/(x + 3), no restriction#The denominator excludes x = -3.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/x - 1/(x + 5). {
~ 0/(x(x + 5))#The +5 remains after subtracting x.
~ 5/(x + 5), x &ne; -5#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
= 5/(x(x + 5)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -5#Correct. The numerator is (x + 5) - x = 5.
~ 1/5#This treats denominators as if they subtract directly.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What restrictions apply to 2/(x<sup>2</sup> - 4) - 1/(x + 2)? {
~ x &ne; 4 and x &ne; -4#The zeros of x<sup>2</sup> - 4 are &plusmn;2.
~ x &ne; -2 only#The factor x - 2 also excludes 2.
~ all real numbers#The denominator is zero at x = 2 and -2.
= x &ne; 2 and x &ne; -2#Correct. x<sup>2</sup> - 4 factors as (x - 2)(x + 2).
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 4/(x + 1) + 2/(x - 1). {
= (6x - 2)/((x + 1)(x - 1)), x &ne; -1 and x &ne; 1#Correct. Compute 4(x - 1) + 2(x + 1) = 6x - 2.
~ (6x + 2)/((x + 1)(x - 1)), x &ne; -1 and x &ne; 1#The 4(x - 1) contributes -4.
~ 6/(2x)#Denominators are not added.
~ (6x - 2)/(x - 1), x &ne; 1#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 5/x - 3/(x + 2). {
~ (2x - 10)/(x(x + 2)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -2#The constant from 5(x + 2) is +10.
= (2x + 10)/(x(x + 2)), x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -2#Correct. Compute 5(x + 2) - 3x = 2x + 10.
~ 2/(2x + 2)#Denominators are not added.
~ (2x + 10)/(x + 2), x &ne; -2#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why must subtraction be distributed in rational expression subtraction? {
~ because denominators are subtracted#Denominators are not subtracted after finding an LCD.
~ because restrictions disappear#Restrictions remain from original denominators.
= the entire second numerator is being subtracted#Correct. The minus sign applies to every term of the rewritten second numerator.
~ because all signs become positive#Subtraction can make some terms negative.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2/(x - 3) + 1/(x<sup>2</sup> - 9). {
~ (2x + 6)/((x - 3)(x + 3)), x &ne; 3 and x &ne; -3#The +1 numerator must be included.
~ 3/(x<sup>2</sup> - 9)#The first numerator must be adjusted by x + 3.
~ (2x + 7)/(x - 3), x &ne; 3#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
= (2x + 7)/((x - 3)(x + 3)), x &ne; 3 and x &ne; -3#Correct. Use LCD (x - 3)(x + 3) and compute 2(x + 3) + 1.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 1/(x - 4) - 1/(x + 4). {
= 8/((x - 4)(x + 4)), x &ne; 4 and x &ne; -4#Correct. The numerator is (x + 4) - (x - 4) = 8.
~ 0/((x - 4)(x + 4))#The constants do not cancel because subtraction distributes.
~ 8/(2x)#Denominators are not added.
~ 1/8#This subtracts denominators instead of forming an LCD.
}

